  Reading your answer back to confirm I have it right: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m learns the [38;5;153muserId[39m from the API response, and then that
  [38;5;153muserId[39m gets stored somewhere durable that other forms can read later. Good — that's coherent, and it's a bigger change
  than the one-parameter version.

  Next question, and it's the one that determines nearly everything downstream:

  [1mThe fork:[0m what the stored [38;5;153muserId[39m is [3mfor[0m decides how much it has to be defended. Two very different things wear the
  same name:

  - [1mCorrelation / analytics identity[0m — you want log lines and events to say which user did what, for debugging and
    product metrics. Nothing grants access based on it. If it's wrong or tampered with, you get bad telemetry, and
    that's the whole blast radius. Cheap, and cheap to change later.
  - [1mSession / auth identity[0m — other forms read it to know who's acting, and behavior depends on it. This puts you in
    security territory: a [38;5;153muserId[39m sitting in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is readable and writable by any script on the page, so it can't
    be the thing that authorizes anything. That constraint shapes the design from the start rather than getting bolted
    on, which is why I'm asking now instead of later.

  [1mMy recommendation: build it as correlation identity, explicitly.[0m Your current [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that doesn't even call
  the API yet, so there's no real auth to hang a session on, and designing a client-side store that [3mlooks[0m like auth
  state is how it quietly becomes load-bearing. If you need real sessions later, that's a server-side token, and it's a
  separate design.
